\begin{abstract}Antimicrobial resistance threatens the effectiveness of standard antibiotic therapy. We test whether combining an efflux pump inhibitor with low-dose beta-lactam treatment slows the emergence of resistance in a simulated population of Escherichia coli. Using a stochastic serial-passage model calibrated on published mutation and efflux parameters, we compare 30 replicate populations under combination therapy against 30 under beta-lactam monotherapy. Combination therapy delayed the median time to resistance fixation from 14 to 22 passages, a statistically significant difference (Mann-Whitney U, p = 0.004) with a large effect size (Cohen's d = 0.82). The 95% confidence interval for the delay was 4.1 to 11.6 passages. These results suggest that efflux inhibition is a promising adjuvant strategy, and they motivate wet-lab validation with clinical isolates.\end{abstract}
\section{Introduction}
Resistance to beta-lactam antibiotics is rising worldwide \cite{pubmed:sample-1}. Efflux pumps help bacteria survive low antibiotic concentrations \cite{pubmed:sample-2}.
\section{Related Work}
Prior modelling studies examined combination therapy \cite{arxiv:sample-3} and mutation supply \cite{pubmed:sample-4}.
\section{Methodology}
We simulate 30 replicate populations per arm with a stochastic serial-passage model.
